    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation",
    "problem": "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=28$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=3.60.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=14,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{3.60}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=14$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14-28}{\\sqrt{3.60}} \\\\  & ≈-7.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.",
